      },
      "worked": "Subtracting the first circle equation from the second gives\n$$\\begin{align*}6x+6y&=r^2-4.\\end{align*}$$\nLet $s=\\frac{r^2-4}{6}$, so $y=s-x$. Substituting into the first circle gives\n$$\\begin{align*}(x-1)^2+(s-x-2)^2&=4\\\\2x^2+(2-2s)x+(s-2)^2-3&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nEach real root determines exactly one common point because $y=s-x$. The discriminant is\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta&=(2-2s)^2-4(2)\\bigl((s-2)^2-3\\bigr)\\\\\\Delta&=32-4(s-3)^2.\\end{align*}$$\nTwo distinct intersections require two distinct real roots, so $\\Delta>0$:\n$$\\begin{align*}32-4(s-3)^2&>0\\\\(s-3)^2&<8\\\\3-2\\sqrt{2}&<s<3+2\\sqrt{2}\\\\3-2\\sqrt{2}&<\\frac{r^2-4}{6}<3+2\\sqrt{2}\\\\22-12\\sqrt{2}&<r^2<22+12\\sqrt{2}\\\\(3\\sqrt{2}-2)^2&<r^2<(3\\sqrt{2}+2)^2.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $r>0$ and $3\\sqrt{2}-2>0$,\n$$3\\sqrt{2}-2<r<3\\sqrt{2}+2.$$\nAt either endpoint the discriminant is zero, giving only one intersection, so both endpoints are excluded.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
